        <body>
            <div xml:id="d87">

                <pb facs="0189" n="159" sameAs="frus:frus1872p2v5/pg159"/>

                <head>No. 60.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#center">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Report of Captain G. H</hi>. <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Richards</hi>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1858-10-03"><hi
                                rendition="#italic">October</hi> 23, 1858</date>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">in papers relating to British Columbia, presented to
                            both Houses of Parliament</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic">by command of
                            Her Majesty</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic">August</hi> 12, 1859.—<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Part II, p.</hi> 14.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>“The Haro Strait lies between Vancouver Island and the principal islands
                    composing the archipelago. * * In the Haro Strait, Cordova Bay on the western or
                    Vancouver shore offers good anchorage. On Stewart Island, which helps to form
                    the eastern side of the strait, there are snug and land-locked harbors, easily
                    accessible to steamers; and among the Saturna group—the western *boundary of the
                    strait where it enters the Gulf of Georgia—there is good shelter for a fleet,
                    accessible either to sailing-vessels or steamers.”<seg type="margin">Description
                        of Haro channel by Capt. Richards, British Boundary Commissioner.</seg><seg
                        type="margin">[77]</seg></p>
            </div>
